---
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import { asset } from "../data/project";
---

<section
  class="demo-section wide"
  id="demo"
  aria-label="HOIST demonstration video"
>
  <div class="video-frame">
    <div class="video-heading">
      <span><span class="status-dot"></span> HOIST in action</span><span
        >Simulation + real humanoid</span
      >
    </div>
    <video
      id="hoist-demo"
      controls
      playsinline
      preload="none"
      poster={asset("media/video-poster.jpg")}
      width="1280"
      height="720"
      aria-label="HOIST research demonstration: method, simulation, and real humanoid experiments"
      aria-describedby="video-description"
    >
      <source src={asset("media/hoist.mp4")} type="video/mp4" />
      <track
        kind="descriptions"
        src={asset("media/hoist-descriptions.vtt")}
        srclang="en"
        label="English visual descriptions"
      />
      Your browser does not support embedded video. <a
        href={asset("media/hoist.mp4")}>Download the demonstration.</a
      >
    </video>
  </div>
  <div class="video-caption">
    <p id="video-description">
      A humanoid learns to approach, guide, and place an externally suspended
      payload.
    </p><a href={asset("media/hoist.mp4")} download
      >Download video <Icon name="ri:arrow-down-line" /></a
    >
  </div>
  <p class="media-kind">Silent video · 1 minute 35 seconds · No spoken audio</p>
  <details class="video-description video-transcript">
    <summary>Read the descriptive video transcript</summary>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <time>00:00–00:04.</time> The title introduces HOIST. A pipeline diagram links
        VR teleoperation, a vision-language-action policy, whole-body control, and
        reinforcement-learning refinement.
      </li>
      <li>
        <time>00:04–00:08.</time> Three panels compare directly actuated pick-and-place,
        an externally suspended payload, and HOIST. The humanoid guides the suspended
        box through contact; the load can swing and overshoot the marked target after
        the robot stops.
      </li>
      <li>
        <time>00:08–00:16.</time> A motivation figure contrasts imitation-only placement
        errors, unsafe exploration with reinforcement learning from scratch, and imitation-initialized
        refinement with HOIST.
      </li>
      <li>
        <time>00:16–00:42.</time> Three synchronized panels show physical humanoid
        experiments: VLA-50 on the left, VLA-80 in the center, and HOIST on the right.
        A humanoid approaches and pushes a gray suspended box as it descends toward
        a rectangular target marked on a wooden platform. The methods end with different
        payload positions. Text identifies each method and states “1× speed” and “fully
        autonomous.”
      </li>
      <li>
        <time>00:42–01:35.</time> The video transitions to repeated simulation trials
        with the same left-to-right method order. Humanoids guide cable-suspended
        boxes toward raised target platforms. The boxes continue moving during contact
        and descent; differences in pushing and stopping produce different final positions.
        <a href="#results">The experiment results below</a> provide the numerical
        placement errors.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <a href={asset("media/hoist-descriptions.vtt")} download
      >Download timed visual descriptions (WebVTT)</a
    >
  </details>
</section>
